399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (My project was an Arduino-based Automatic Transformer Protection System designed to protect the transformer and connected load from abnormal operating conditions. We monitored four parameters: voltage, current and temperature. For voltage measurement, we used a ZMPT101B voltage-sensing module, and for current measurement, we used an ACS712 current sensor. An LM35 temperature sensor was used to monitor the transformer temperature.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (The sensor outputs were connected to the Arduino Uno, which acts as the main controller. The Arduino processes the sensor signals, calculates the corresponding voltage, current and temperature values, and compares them with predefined safe limits. If the voltage exceeds the upper limit, an overvoltage fault is detected; if it falls below the lower limit, an undervoltage fault is detected. Similarly, excessive current produces an overcurrent fault, and excessive temperature produces an overtemperature fault.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (When any fault is detected, the Arduino de-energizes a relay to disconnect the load, while a buzzer and red LED provide an alarm and the LCD displays the fault condition. The fault is latched so that the load does not automatically reconnect. After the abnormal condition is cleared, the operator can press a manual reset button, and the Arduino first verifies that all parameters are within safe limits before reconnecting the load.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
402 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (The main principle of the project is: sensing, processing, fault detection, relay isolation and indication.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
